//! `pulse-wire` Node.js bindings.
//!
//! Wraps `pulse_wire_udp::{Sender, Receiver}` and the core `Pulse`
//! type so JavaScript / TypeScript callers can drive PMS Tier-1 +
//! Tier-2 framed UDP traffic without dropping into native code.
//!
//! Built with napi-rs + `@napi-rs/cli`. The native module is loaded
//! by `index.js` (auto-generated by `napi build`) which in turn
//! bridges into `package.json`'s `main` field.
//!
//! ## API surface
//!
//! - `Pulse` (class) — 128-byte wire-format Pulse with field
//!   accessors and bytes round-trip.
//! - `Sender` (class) — `bind`, `connect`, `send`, `sendTo`.
//! - `Receiver` (class) — `bind`, `setReadTimeoutMs`, `recv`,
//!   `recvInto`, and `recvAsync`, which waits off the event loop.
//! - `bindPair` — a `Sender` and a `Receiver` on one UDP socket.
//! - `Channel` (class, `channel.rs`) — messages between processes on one
//!   host over the shared-memory ring and envelope store.
//! - `encodeEnvelope`, `decodeEnvelope`, `envelopeId` (`envelope.rs`) —
//!   the canonical `PulseEnvelope` codec.
//! - Module-level constants `PULSE_WIRE_SIZE`, `MAX_ENVELOPE`,
//!   `HEADER_BYTES`.
//! - `Instruction` (enum) — `Null`, `Heartbeat`, `Data`.
//!
//! ## Waiting
//!
//! `Receiver.recv`, `recvInto`, and `Channel`'s `send`, `recv` and `open`
//! wait on the calling thread, which in Node is the event loop: no timer,
//! I/O callback or promise runs until they return. `Receiver.recvAsync` and
//! `Channel.recvAsync` wait on a thread of the object's own (`waiter.rs`)
//! and return a promise. While one has not settled, the object's
//! synchronous receives throw `Busy` instead of waiting for the lock it
//! holds, which would stall the event loop.
//!
//! ## Errors
//!
//! Every error carries a `code` (`error.rs`): napi's `InvalidArg` and
//! `GenericFailure`, or `Timeout`, `Busy`, `ChannelClosed`,
//! `EnvelopeTooLarge` and `EnvelopeIdMismatch`.
//!
//! ## Memory model
//!
//! All boundary types map to their JS equivalents:
//! - `String` <-> `String`
//! - `Vec<u8>` / `&[u8]` <-> `Buffer`
//! - `u64` <-> `BigInt` (preserves precision past 2^53)
//! - `u32` <-> `number`

